3. Reverse-engineering material and game content
================================================================================

`gamedata/tankrun_firstrock.txt` contains a 40-byte x86 instruction pattern
with wildcards, used by the Dynamic Hooks extension to locate
`CTankClaw::PrimaryAttack` in a specific build of `server.dll`.

`gamedata/tankrun_wipe.txt` contains a single structure member offset
(`CDirector::m_RestartTimer = 616` / `0x268`) for the same Windows x86 build
of `server.dll`, verified against `CDirector::EndScenario` (VA `0x10269079`)
and `CDirector::UpdateScenarioState` (VA `0x10271bfd`). Runtime code validates
both duration (~7.0s) and remaining time (~7.0s) before writing and falls back
safely to stock behavior on any mismatch.

`gamedata/tankrun_rocktarget.txt` contains a 43-byte x86 instruction pattern
and two verified integer offsets (`TankAttack::m_hTarget = 52` / `0x34` and
`TankAttack::ThrowRockHelper_ReadTargetRel = 216` / `0xd8`) used by a read-only
Dynamic Hooks detour on `TankAttack::ThrowRockHelper` (VA `0x10472d00`) for the
same Windows x86 build of `server.dll` (`PatchVersion 2.2.4.3`, size `9,130,288`,
SHA-256 `a93ec916a8f26fbea85688c321d2ba8dd346d435fab09a0b926dd950c2e1ea12`).
Runtime code performs strict OS, signature, offset, and read-only entry/internal
instruction byte checks before enabling the detour and disables the native rock
target glow path safely on any mismatch.

* It contains no game code, data files, assets, symbols or decompiled source.
* It was derived from static analysis of a legitimately installed copy of the
  game, for interoperability and modding purposes.
* It is a short functional pattern rather than an expressive work. It is
  nevertheless disclosed here so that redistributors can make their own
  assessment.
* The pattern is build-bound: the corresponding `server.dll` SHA256 is recorded
  in the file and in `docs/compatibility/32-bit-baseline.md`.

This repository does NOT contain:
    - game binaries, VPK archives, models, materials or other game assets;
    - decompiled code, disassembly dumps or analysis database exports;
    - third-party plugin source code or binaries.

